.nh
.SH NAME
ktm-guide-errors \- Errors (chapter 15 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
ktm reports errors with exceptions: \fBthrow\fR stops what is running and jumps to the nearest \fBtry\fR that encloses it, any number of calls up, where \fBcatch\fR handles the error. This chapter covers the \fBError\fR struct, throwing, catching, error codes, rethrowing, what happens on the way up, errors the library throws, \fB@caller\fR parameters for saying where a problem is, and the report an uncaught error prints.
.SH The Error struct
.PP
Every thrown error is an \fBError\fR\&. Every program has the struct, from the prelude:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Error {
    i64 code;       /* what went wrong: usually an enum member */
    u8 *message;    /* what to tell a person, "" when there is none */
    u8 *file;       /* where the throw is */
    i32 line;
};
.EE
.RE
.PP
The code is for the program to decide what to do; the message is for people. The compiler fills in the file and line of the \fBthrow\fR\&.
.SH Throwing and catching
.PP
\fBthrow(code)\fR throws an error with a code and an empty message. \fBthrow(code, format, ...)\fR adds a message, formatted like \fBprintf\fR\&. \fBtry { ... } catch (Error e) { ... }\fR runs the block and, if anything in it throws, the catch block with the error in \fBe\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum BankError { funds, locked };

void withdraw(i64 balance, i64 amount) {
    if (amount > balance) {
        throw(BankError.funds, "need %d, have %d", amount, balance);
    }
    printf("withdrew %d\en", amount);
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        withdraw(100, 30);
        withdraw(100, 500);
        printf("never printed\en");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("error %d: %s\en", e.code, e.message);
    }
    printf("carrying on\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
withdrew 30
error 0: need 500, have 100
carrying on
.EE
.RE
.PP
The code after the throw in the \fBtry\fR block doesn't run; the program continues after the \fBcatch\fR block.
.PP
The error carries where it was thrown:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { bad };

i32 main() {
    try {
        throw(Err.bad, "something broke");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s:%d: %s\en", e.file, e.line, e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
example.ktm:5: something broke
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without a message, \fBe.message\fR is the empty string, never \fBnull\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { bad };

i32 main() {
    try {
        throw(Err.bad);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("code %d, message [%s]\en", e.code, e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
code 0, message []
.EE
.RE
.PP
The message's format is checked like \fBprintf\fR\&'s: it must get as many arguments as it has \fB%\fR conversions.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { bad };

i32 main() {
    throw(Err.bad, "need %d, have %d", 500);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
format expects 2 arguments but 1 was supplied
.EE
.RE
.SS Catching without the error
.PP
When the error itself doesn't matter, \fBcatch\fR takes no binding:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { any };

i32 main() {
    try {
        throw(Err.any);
    } catch {
        printf("something went wrong\en");
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
something went wrong
.EE
.RE
.SH Error codes
.PP
The code is an integer. An enum per kind of failure keeps codes readable, and lets the catch decide by comparing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum ParseError { empty, notNumber, tooBig };

i64 parse(u8[] s) {
    if (!s.len) {
        throw(ParseError.empty, "empty input");
    }
    i64 n = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < s.len; i++) {
        if (s[i] < '0' || s[i] > '9') {
            throw(ParseError.notNumber, "bad digit '%c'", s[i]);
        }
        n = n * 10 + s[i] \- '0';
        if (n > 1000) {
            throw(ParseError.tooBig, "%d is over 1000", n);
        }
    }
    return n;
}

void tryParse(u8[] s) {
    try {
        printf("%d\en", parse(s));
    } catch (Error e) {
        if (e.code == ParseError.empty) {
            printf("(nothing)\en");
        } else {
            printf("rejected: %s\en", e.message);
        }
    }
}

i32 main() {
    tryParse("42");
    tryParse("");
    tryParse("4x");
    tryParse("99999");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
(nothing)
rejected: bad digit 'x'
rejected: 9999 is over 1000
.EE
.RE
.PP
The code converts like an argument to an integer parameter: a float is truncated.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    try {
        throw(42.75);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%d\en", e.code);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
.EE
.RE
.PP
The library's modules each have their own codes, \fBpub const\fRs named \fBERR_...\fR in the module (\fBjson.ERR_DECODE\fR, \fBcrypto.ERR_CRYPTO\fR); \fBERR_BOUNDS\fR is in the prelude. They are large numbers chosen so that different modules never share one. A library of your own can do the same: one \fBERR_\fR constant per kind of failure, with values unlikely to clash.
.SH Through any number of calls
.PP
A throw leaves every function between it and the \fBtry\fR: none of them returns normally, and the rest of their code doesn't run. Their \fBdefer\fR statements do run, innermost first, on the way up (see defer and scoped (see ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { bad };

void level3() {
    throw(Err.bad, "deep down");
}

void level2() {
    defer printf("level2 cleans up\en");
    level3();
    printf("not printed\en");
}

void level1() {
    defer printf("level1 cleans up\en");
    level2();
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        level1();
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("caught: %s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
level2 cleans up
level1 cleans up
caught: deep down
.EE
.RE
.PP
The nearest enclosing \fBtry\fR catches; the outer ones don't see the error:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { inner };

i32 main() {
    try {
        try {
            throw(Err.inner, "first");
        } catch (Error e) {
            printf("inner catch: %s\en", e.message);
        }
        printf("after the inner try\en");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("outer catch\en");
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
inner catch: first
after the inner try
.EE
.RE
.PP
A throw inside a \fBcatch\fR block goes to the next \fBtry\fR out:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { first, second };

i32 main() {
    try {
        try {
            throw(Err.first);
        } catch {
            throw(Err.second, "while handling the first");
        }
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%d: %s\en", e.code, e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1: while handling the first
.EE
.RE
.SH Rethrowing
.PP
\fBthrow(e)\fR throws the caught error on, unchanged: the same code, message, file and line. A handler that only deals with some errors passes the others up this way:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { notFound, denied };

void open(u8 *path) {
    if (path[0] != '/') {
        throw(Err.notFound, "%s: no such file", path);
    }
    throw(Err.denied, "%s: permission denied", path);
}

void openOrSkip(u8 *path) {
    try {
        open(path);
    } catch (Error e) {
        if (e.code == Err.notFound) {
            printf("skipping: %s\en", e.message);
            return;
        }
        throw(e);
    }
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        openOrSkip("notes.txt");
        openOrSkip("/etc/shadow");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("fatal: %s (line %d)\en", e.message, e.line);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
skipping: notes.txt: no such file
fatal: /etc/shadow: permission denied (line 7)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The line is 7, the original \fBthrow\fR in \fBopen\fR, not the \fBthrow(e)\fR\&.
.PP
To add context on the way up, throw a new error whose message includes the old one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { io, config };

void readFile(u8 *path) {
    throw(Err.io, "%s: no such file", path);
}

void loadConfig() {
    try {
        readFile("app.conf");
    } catch (Error e) {
        throw(Err.config, "loading the configuration: %s", e.message);
    }
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        loadConfig();
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%d: %s\en", e.code, e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1: loading the configuration: app.conf: no such file
.EE
.RE
.SH The message belongs to the catch
.PP
\fBe.message\fR is freed when the \fBcatch\fR block ends. To keep it longer, copy it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import cstr, mem;

enum Err { bad };

i32 main() {
    u8 *kept = null;
    try {
        throw(Err.bad, "worth keeping %d", 42);
    } catch (Error e) {
        kept = cstr.dup(e.message);
    }
    printf("%s\en", kept);
    mem.free(kept);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
worth keeping 42
.EE
.RE
.SH Leaving a try early
.PP
\fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR and \fBgoto\fR may leave a \fBtry\fR block at any depth. A \fBtry\fR left that way stops catching at once:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Err { late };

i64 find(i64[] items, i64 want) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {
        try {
            if (items[i] == want) {
                return i;
            }
            if (items[i] < 0) {
                break;
            }
        } catch {
        }
    }
    return \-1;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 items[4] = {3, 7, \-1, 9};
    try {
        printf("%d %d\en", find(items, 7), find(items, 9));
        throw(Err.late);
    } catch {
        printf("caught here, not in find\en");
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 \-1
caught here, not in find
.EE
.RE
.SH Errors the library throws
.PP
Library functions throw too: an index out of range, text that isn't valid JSON, a network failure. Each module documents its codes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json, vec;

struct Config { i64 port; };

i32 main() {
    vec.Vec<i64> v = vec.new<i64>();
    try {
        v.get(5);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%d: %s\en", e.code == ERR_BOUNDS, e.message);
    }
    try {
        Config c = json.decode<Config>("{\e"port\e": \e"eighty\e"}");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%d: %s\en", e.code == json.ERR_DECODE, e.message);
    }
    v.free();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1: vec.Vec.get: index 5 of 0
1: 1:10: port: expected an integer, got a string
.EE
.RE
.PP
A slice index out of range throws \fBERR_BOUNDS\fR as well (see Arrays and slices (see ktm-guide-arrays-and-slices(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    i64 nums[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    i64[] s = nums;
    i64 i = 3;
    try {
        printf("%d\en", s[i]);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
index 3 out of range for length 3
.EE
.RE
.SH Saying where: @caller
.PP
A helper that checks something and throws reports the helper's own line, which is rarely the interesting one. A parameter marked \fB@caller("file")\fR, \fB@caller("line")\fR or \fB@caller("func")\fR is filled in by every call with where that call is written, so the helper can say where it was called from (see Functions (see ktm-guide-functions(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Check { failed };

void ensure(bool ok, u8 *what, @caller("file") u8 *file, @caller("line") i64 line) {
    if (!ok) {
        throw(Check.failed, "%s:%d: %s", file, line, what);
    }
}

i64 divide(i64 a, i64 b) {
    ensure(b != 0, "divide by zero");
    return a / b;
}

i32 main() {
    try {
        printf("%d\en", divide(10, 2));
        printf("%d\en", divide(1, 0));
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5
example.ktm:10: divide by zero
.EE
.RE
.PP
The caller never passes these parameters, and they can't have default values.
.SH Uncaught errors
.PP
An error that no \fBtry\fR catches, in any thread, ends the program with exit status 1. ktm prints it on stderr: its code, its message, where it was thrown, and the calls that led there.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 balance = 100;

void withdraw(i64 amount) {
    if (amount > balance) {
        throw(0, "need %d, have %d", amount, balance);
    }
    balance \-= amount;
}

i32 main() {
    withdraw(30);
    withdraw(500);
    printf("not printed\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
uncaught error 0: need 500, have 70 (example.ktm:5)
  at example.ktm:5 in withdraw
  called from example.ktm:12 in main
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
What the program printed before comes first (its output is flushed).
.IP \(bu 2
A code whose bytes are all printable characters is shown as text, so a code like \fB0x6B6F6F42\fR prints as \fB\&'Book'\fR; any other is shown as a number.
.IP \(bu 2
The calls are those of the last \fBthrow\fR: an error caught and thrown on with \fBthrow(e)\fR shows where it was thrown on, while the first line keeps the file and line of its first throw.
.IP \(bu 2
The calls are listed the way a crash lists them (see Safety and debugging (see ktm-guide-safety-and-debugging(7))), compiled and in the JIT alike.
.SH Threads and coroutines
.PP
Each thread has its own chain of \fBtry\fR blocks: a throw is caught only by a \fBtry\fR of its own thread, and an uncaught error in any thread ends the whole program. A \fBtry\fR may span a coroutine's \fByield\fR\&. See Threads and threadlocal (see ktm-guide-threads-and-threadlocal(7)).
.SH When to throw
.PP
Throw for what the caller can't reasonably check first and usually can't fix on the spot: a file that is missing, input that isn't valid, a connection that failed. For an outcome the caller expects and branches on every time ("not in the map", "end of input"), return a value: a \fBbool\fR, \fB\-1\fR, or \fBnull\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bool findIndex(i64[] items, i64 want, i64 *at) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {
        if (items[i] == want) {
            *at = i;
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 items[3] = {4, 8, 15};
    i64 at;
    if (findIndex(items, 8, &at)) {
        printf("found at %d\en", at);
    }
    if (!findIndex(items, 99, &at)) {
        printf("not there\en");
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
found at 1
not there
.EE
.RE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthrow(code)\fR and \fBthrow(code, fmt, ...)\fR throw an \fBError\fR; the format's argument count is checked like \fBprintf\fR\&'s, the code converts to an integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtry { } catch (Error e) { }\fR catches errors thrown anywhere inside, any number of calls down; \fBcatch { }\fR catches without binding the error.
.IP \(bu 2
The nearest enclosing \fBtry\fR catches. A throw in a \fBcatch\fR block goes to the next one out.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthrow(e)\fR rethrows the error unchanged.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBe.message\fR is never \fBnull\fR and is freed when the catch block ends.
.IP \(bu 2
Defers run on the way up; \fBreturn\fR, \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR and \fBgoto\fR may leave a \fBtry\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@caller("file")\fR, \fB("line")\fR, \fB("func")\fR parameters are filled in by each call.
.IP \(bu 2
An uncaught error prints its code, message, place and calls on stderr and exits with 1.
.IP \(bu 2
Each thread has its own \fBtry\fR chain.
